ref='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "34500"} "1936"] "\n" [:option {:value "900070"} "1952"] "\n" [:option {:value "34518"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "34572"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "34626"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "34684"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "34742"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "34809"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "34877"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "34945"} "2000"] "\n" [:option {:value "260000"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "314000"} "2012"] "\n" [:option {:value "395600"} "2016"] "\n" [:option {:value "19001471"} "2020"] "\n" [:option {:value "2002500"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/335"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Handball, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "14 – 29 August 2004"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/781"} "Kleisto Gipedo, Olympiako Syngrotima Ellenikou, Hellinikon"] " / " [:a {:href "/venues/802"} "Kleisto Gymnastirio Falirou, Olympiako Syngrotima Papaliakis Zonis Falirou, Faliro"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/35038"} "179 from 12 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Round-robin pools advance teams to classification matches."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The 12 men’s teams qualified for the 2004 Olympics as follows – Greece as the host nation; the top seven teams from the 2003 World Championships (Croatia, Germany, France, Spain, Russia, Hungary, Iceland); the top team from 2004 European Championships, not otherwise qualified (Slovenia, which finished second); Brazil as the 2003 Pan American Games Champion; and the winners of the 2003 Asian and African Olympic Qualifying Tournaments (South Korea and Egypt, respectively)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the round-robin phase, World Championship medallists Croatia (Gorup A) and France (Group B) survived without defeat. Finalist Germany also made it into the quarter-finals, but had incurred losses against Hungary and France. The Sydney champions, Russia, had seen a poor start of their campaign, beating only tiny Slovenia and Iceland, narrowly making it into the next round. Pitted against the French in the quarter-finals, they were soon three points down. But the Russian team picked itself up in the second half, and two late goals opened up a two-goal difference that remained in place until the final whistle (26-24). The other quarter-finals went as expected with Hungary, Croatia and Germany completing the all-European semi-final line-up."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the semi-finals, the Russians were defeated by the strong German defense, losing in a low-scoring 21-15 match. Croatia and Hungary kept it close, the World Champions making it into the final after 33-31. The final then came down to a re-match of the 2003 Worlds, and with the same outcome. The Germans didn’t make it easy for Croatia, leading 12-11 at half-time. The two nations remained neck-on-neck until the 55th minute, when " [:a {:href "/athletes/108640"} "Mirza Džomba"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/108641"} "Nikša Kaleb"] " opened up a two-goal lead that proved decisive. The Russians, meanwhile, took the bronze in a 28-26 win over Hungary."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button708256", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button708256').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {}] [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Team"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Games"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Goals / Saves"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "spli